<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yugurs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yellow Uyghurs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yugurs, also called Yellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, are an ethnic group of about 14,000 people living mainly in Sunan Yugur Autonomous County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. They are commonly regarded as descendants of the Ganzhou Uyghurs, who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> after the fall of the Uyghur Khaganate in 840. Some speak a Turkic language and others a Mongolic one, and most follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yugurs.jpg" alt="Yugurs" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ralph repo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Family_In_Lanchow,_China_1944_Fr._Mark_Tennien_Restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Yugurs, traditionally known as Yellow Uyghurs, are a Turko-Mongol people and one of the 56 ethnic groups officially recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Their own names are Sarığ yoğır in the western group and Shera yogor in the eastern. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 14,706 of them, living mainly in the Sunan Yugur Autonomous County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. The Turkic-speaking Yugurs are considered descendants of a group of Old Uyghurs who fled south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> to Gansu after the Uyghur Khaganate collapsed in 840.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> those <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> established the Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom, which lasted from 870 to 1036, with its capital near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangye">Zhangye</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains">Qilian Mountains</a> in the valley of the Ejin River. In 1037 the Yugurs came under Tangut rule. After Khizr Khoja's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Qumul</a>, residents who rejected conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> fled to nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper, and these are regarded as the ancestors of the Yugurs, who have remained Vajrayana Buddhists to the present day.</p><p>In the late 16th century they adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Sonam Gyatso</a>, the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>. In 1893 the Russian explorer Grigory Potanin, the first Western scientist to study them, published a small glossary of Yugur words with notes on their administration and geography.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Yugurs speak two languages of different families. About 4,600 speak Western Yugur, a Siberian Turkic language that has preserved many archaisms of Old Uyghur, and about 2,800 speak Eastern Yugur, a Mongolic language. Mongolic and Chinese are also used in the eastern provinces. Both Yugur languages are now unwritten, although the Old Uyghur alphabet remained in use in some Yugur communities until the end of the 17th century. Most Yugurs are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhists</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yugurs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Uyghur remnants survived in the region; the <b>Yugur</b> (Yellow Uyghur) people of Gansu are commonly regarded as their descendants.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganzhou-uyghur-kingdom">Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
